# P15: example B1 with a neutron beam, the like-for-like Geant4 column now that the inelastic
# sub-process is wired. The partner is `p15_neutron_port.mac`; both run through
# `ref/b1neutron/run.bat`, which is B1 with `EnableNeutronGeneralProcess` forced false.
#
# WHY A NEW FILE RATHER THAN AN EDIT OF `stage1_neutron.mac`. That macro carries
# `/process/inactivate neutronInelastic`, and it is right for the stage it names: from P8d to
# P15 the port refused the neutron's inelastic final state by name
# (`HadronicRefusal::kNeutronInelastic`) and a column that left it active on the Geant4 side
# would have been comparing two different physics lists. P15 wires it, so the line comes off -
# and the old pair is kept unedited because the numbers already published against it were taken
# with it.
#
# WHY THE GENERAL PROCESS IS STILL OFF ON BOTH SIDES. It is not a physics choice and it is not
# the port's doing: with `EnableNeutronGeneralProcess = 1` a neutron's manager holds exactly
# `Transportation`, `Decay` and `NeutronGeneralProc`, so `/process/inactivate neutronInelastic`
# answers `illegal process (or type) name` and NOTHING inside the general process can be reached
# by name at all (docs/RISK.md V53). Turning it off is what makes the five ion inactivations
# below expressible; the sub-processes it splits into are the same code.
#
# WHAT IS STILL INACTIVATED, and it is the same list the sweep uses:
#
#   dInelastic, tInelastic, He3Inelastic, alphaInelastic, ionInelastic
#                   P9e's `G4BinaryLightIonReaction::Interact` is not wired, so the port refuses
#                   every ion interaction at or above 50 MeV per nucleon. The PORT holds the
#                   same five off through `G4GPU_ION_INELASTIC=0` - a process inactivated on one
#                   side only is not a like-for-like column in either direction, and that is
#                   exactly the defect docs/RISK.md V192 records.
#   ionElastic      `G4NuclNuclDiffuseElastic`, both halves ported and the channel not wired.
#
# A 100 MeV neutron beam is the case the two groups of the refusal ledger were built for: the
# neutron itself has a full inelastic process now, and what it MAKES - deuterons, tritons,
# alphas and recoil nuclides - is what the five names above cover.

/run/initialize
/control/verbose 0
/run/verbose 1

/process/inactivate dInelastic
/process/inactivate tInelastic
/process/inactivate He3Inelastic
/process/inactivate alphaInelastic
/process/inactivate ionInelastic
/process/inactivate ionElastic

# The stage, recorded by what RAN and not by what was intended: Active/InActive for every
# process on the neutron's manager, after the inactivations above. `/process/inactivate` ignores
# a name the species does not have (docs/RISK.md V43), so this dump is the only thing that makes
# the list above a claim rather than a hope.
/particle/select neutron
/particle/process/dump

/gun/particle neutron
/gun/energy 100 MeV

/run/printProgress 100000
/run/beamOn 500000
